[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa4d3460-2748-5ad3-bd8b-990ea542e91d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa4d3460-2748-5ad3-bd8b-990ea542e91d" :question/id #uuid "aa4d3460-2748-5ad3-bd8b-990ea542e91d" :question/math-academy-id "q-10870" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=3(2)^{2}=12,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-8 & =-\\frac{1}{12}(x-(2)) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{12}x+\\frac{1}{6}+8 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{12}x+\\frac{49}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{12}x+\\frac{49}{6} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{12}x & =\\frac{49}{6} \\\\ x & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(98,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59b21907-6a2e-5305-bbec-3d26d82d800a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59b21907-6a2e-5305-bbec-3d26d82d800a" :answer-field/id #uuid "59b21907-6a2e-5305-bbec-3d26d82d800a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90c5e6df-7242-5471-add4-9e72fb15748c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6ace2d5c-ca5d-52fc-8f62-fbef69d9fba6" "answer-a5f8e7b1-4e1e-50a9-a92e-4620063f5c00" "answer-c8a6ad07-90a6-5d02-b9eb-b5a27c586716" "answer-1b2a6764-00bd-5f28-952f-20dd216fef53" "answer-90c5e6df-7242-5471-add4-9e72fb15748c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field